```javascript
/**
 * Removes duplicate customer records, keeping the first occurrence.
 * Two records are duplicates if they share an email (case-insensitive)
 * or a phone number (digits only). Names are not compared, since
 * different customers can share a name.
 */
function removeDuplicateCustomers(customers) {
  const seenEmails = new Set();
  const seenPhones = new Set();

  return customers.filter(({ email, phone }) => {
    const e = (email ?? "").trim().toLowerCase();
    const p = (phone ?? "").replace(/\D/g, "");

    if ((e && seenEmails.has(e)) || (p && seenPhones.has(p))) {
      return false;
    }

    if (e) seenEmails.add(e);
    if (p) seenPhones.add(p);
    return true;
  });
}

// Example
const customers = [
  { name: "Ann Lee", email: "ann@example.com", phone: "555-123-4567" },
  { name: "Ann L.", email: "ANN@example.com ", phone: "(555) 987-6543" }, // same email
  { name: "Bob Ray", email: "bob@example.com", phone: "555.123.4567" },   // same phone as Ann
  { name: "Cy Poe", email: "cy@example.com", phone: "555-000-1111" },
];

console.log(removeDuplicateCustomers(customers));
// -> Ann Lee and Cy Poe
```

Design choices you may want to change:
- **Match rule:** a match on email or phone counts as a duplicate. To require both, build a single key such as `${e}|${p}` and use one `Set`.
- **Which record wins:** the first one is kept. To keep the last, reverse the array before and after filtering.
- **Normalization:** emails are trimmed and lowercased, and phones are reduced to digits. Records with a blank email or phone are never matched on that field.
- **Chained matches:** if A matches B on email and B matches C on phone, but A and C share nothing, C is kept. Catching that needs a union-find approach, which I can add.

